<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alhambra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alhambra is a palace and fortress complex on the Sabika hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Begun by the Nasrid founder Muhammad I in the 13th century, its finest palaces, including the Court of the Lions, were built under Yusuf I and Muhammad V in the 14th century, decorated with carved stucco, tilework and poems on its walls. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="Alhambra" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jebulon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawn_Charles_V_Palace_Alhambra_Granada_Andalusia_Spain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Alhambra stands on Sabika hill, an outcrop of the Sierra Nevada that had been the site of earlier fortresses and of the 11th-century palace of Samuel ibn Naghrillah. The first reference to the Qal'at al-Ḥamra comes from the rule of 'Abdallah ibn Muhammad (888-912), when the castle was small and its walls could not deter an army. The name means the red one, after the reddish colour of its walls of rammed earth, which owes its hue to iron oxide in the local clay. Building of the present complex began in 1238 under Muhammad I Ibn al-Ahmar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the Nasrid period the Alhambra was a self-contained city separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> below, with a Friday mosque, hammams, roads, houses, workshops, a tannery and a sophisticated water supply. It had at least six major palaces, of which the Mexuar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comares-palace">Comares Palace</a>, the Palace of the Lions and the Partal Palace are the best preserved. The Alcazaba fortress stands at its western tip, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalife">Generalife</a>, a former summer palace, lies outside the walls. The site is about 700 to 740 metres long.</p><p>The palaces are organised around rectangular courtyards with a pool, fountain or water channel at the centre. Decoration was concentrated inside, in tile mosaics on lower walls and carved stucco above, with geometric patterns, plant motifs, Arabic inscriptions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas">muqarnas</a> vaulting. The main gate, the Puerta de la Justicia, was built in 1348 under Yusuf I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Christian conquest of 1492 the site became the Royal Court of Ferdinand and Isabella, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> received royal endorsement for his expedition. In 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> commissioned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace beside the Nasrid ones. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops destroyed parts in 1812. Washington Irving's Tales of the Alhambra (1832) brought it international attention, and an Alhambra-inspired style called Alhambresque became popular in the West after Owen Jones published his plans of the Alhambra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1842 to 1845. M. C. Escher visited in 1922, and his study of the symmetries of the tiles, which contain nearly all of the seventeen possible wallpaper groups, inspired his work on tessellation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alhambra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The walls of the <b>Alhambra</b> are inscribed with poems by the court poet Ibn Zamrak, some of which speak in the voice of the building itself.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In architecture the Marinids refined the Almoravid and Almohad legacy with ever more elaborate wood and stucco and were the first to use zellij mosaic tilework extensively, which became standard in Moroccan building; their style was closely akin to the Nasrid <b>Alhambra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Islamic architecture produced the Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem, completed in 691–692, the Great Mosque of Córdoba, the <b>Alhambra</b> of Granada, the mosques of Isfahan and Istanbul and the Taj Mahal.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Alhambra</b> Decree gave Jews four months to convert or leave.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-31">31 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
